          "source_quote": "ἀνωφέλητα μετανοήσουσιν",
          "english_quote": "they will reorient themselves to no avail",
          "issue": "This is a possible interpretation, but the rationale applies the reorientation terminology without disclosing the contextual alternative of futile regret or retrospective change of mind under punishment. The contrast with the present opportunity for μετάνοια does not conclusively determine whether the future response is moral-spiritual redirection or regret. The policy expressly allows context-supported exceptions.",
          "suggested_revision": "Add an explicit alternative such as “they will regret it to no avail,” explaining that the postmortem response may mean futile repentance or regret. Retain “reorient themselves” only as a documented interpretive choice, not as an unambiguous lexical result. Any changed main-text wording requires fresh review.",
          "severity": "major"
        },
        {
          "source_quote": "ὡς ὅτι υἱὸς θεοῦ ἐπιδεδήμηκεν τῇ Ἰουδαίᾳ",
          "english_quote": "namely, that the Son of God has come to Judea",
          "issue": "The reported-content interpretation is defensible, but the explanatory or causal force of ὡς ὅτι and the boundary of the imagined quotation remain uncertain. The existing source issue appropriately discloses this; neither “namely, that” nor the extended quotation should be treated as conclusively established.",
          "suggested_revision": "Retain the explicit alternative in source_issues and keep the chosen discourse attachment and quotation boundary provisional. A grammatical justification or any replacement punctuation must receive fresh review. This is interpretive uncertainty, not demonstrated textual corruption or an OCR defect.",
          "severity": "minor"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "reference": "1.8",
